/P 152 0 R
/E (  First, it makes communication easier. You stop waiting for perfect understanding before you act. You say what you mean as clearly as you can, then you let go. You know some people will misunderstand. That's not your failure. That's human limitation. So you build buffer into important things. You get things in writing. You check for understanding without being defensive about it.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (  Second, it makes you less reactive to misunderstanding. Someone gets mad about something you said. Your first move isn't to defend or explain further. It's to pause and ask: what did they actually hear? Often it's not what you meant. But if you assume that's normal instead of unusual, you can respond helpfully instead of defensively.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
